\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The boundary law for balanced equal-variance phases under Theorem~\ref {thm:physical-boundary}'s moment and exceptional-mass hypotheses, evaluated from the limiting formula (not finite-size Potts data). At $\lambda _*=2\Phi ^{-1}(3/4)\simeq 1.34898$, the best calibration that retains both phases and the one that discards a phase have the same limiting error. The same curve holds for the exact scalar Gaussian model of Proposition~\ref {prop:scalar-gaussian}.}}{29}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:boundary-optimization}{{2}{29}{The boundary law for balanced equal-variance phases under Theorem~\ref {thm:physical-boundary}'s moment and exceptional-mass hypotheses, evaluated from the limiting formula (not finite-size Potts data). At $\lambda _*=2\Phi ^{-1}(3/4)\simeq 1.34898$, the best calibration that retains both phases and the one that discards a phase have the same limiting error. The same curve holds for the exact scalar Gaussian model of Proposition~\ref {prop:scalar-gaussian}}{figure.2}{}}
